1008:main.c        **** 	//The USI counter has overflowed... this occurs in a couple cases..
1009:main.c        **** 	// First: After the address and r/w bit have been received
1010:main.c        **** 	// Second: After the ACK bit has been transmitted (by the slave)
1011:main.c        **** 	// Third: After the first data-byte has been received
1012:main.c        **** 	// Fourth: After the ACK bit has been transmitted for it...
1013:main.c        **** 	// and so on...
1014:main.c        **** 
1015:main.c        **** 	//The counter-overflow also pulls SCL Low (from the manual):
1016:main.c        **** 	// After eight bits containing slave address and data direction 
1017:main.c        **** 	// (read or write) have been transferred, the slave counter overflows 
1018:main.c        **** 	// and the SCL line is forced low. 
1019:main.c        **** 
1020:main.c        **** 	// If the slave is not the one the 
1021:main.c        **** 	// master has addressed, it releases the SCL line and waits for a new 
1022:main.c        **** 	// start condition.
1023:main.c        **** 
1024:main.c        **** 
1025:main.c        **** 	//These cases are the same as AWAITING, except if we're here,
